export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"chip","name":"Chip design","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"chip-overview","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: what the add-on is","summary":"Design a small digital chip from Verilog to layout in CadCraft, with tools you install yourself, kept as a project you can run again.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["chip design","asic","hdl","verilog","vhdl","rtl","synthesis","place and route","gds","layout","open silicon","pdk","eda","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"the-flow","text":"The flow","level":2},{"id":"your-tools-run-as-separate-programs","text":"Your tools, run as separate programs","level":2},{"id":"what-it-is-not","text":"What it is not","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChip design\u003C/strong\u003E is an add-on for CadCraft (Settings › Add-ons › Chip design; off until you switch it on). It keeps a chip design as a \u003Cstrong\u003Eproject\u003C/strong\u003E: your Verilog or VHDL files, the stages of the flow, the tools and their versions, and for every run the SHA-256 of what went in and what came out, so you can see when a run would be the same and run it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-flow\"\u003EThe flow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESource\u003C/strong\u003E — write and check Verilog or VHDL; errors at their line; the module \u003Cstrong\u003EHierarchy\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESimulation\u003C/strong\u003E — your testbench with Icarus Verilog or Verilator; the waves in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWaves\u003C/strong\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESynthesis\u003C/strong\u003E — Yosys maps the design onto a cell library (a Liberty file); cells, area and timing in Problems; the gate-level \u003Cstrong\u003ENetlist\u003C/strong\u003E as a schematic.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlace and route\u003C/strong\u003E — OpenROAD with your LEF, Liberty and SDC; the \u003Cstrong\u003EFloorplan\u003C/strong\u003E tab shows the DEF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayout\u003C/strong\u003E — GDSII and OASIS files, layers and cell hierarchy; \u003Cstrong\u003ECell library and floorplan\u003C/strong\u003E; \u003Cstrong\u003EConstraints and timing data\u003C/strong\u003E (SDC, SPEF, SDF).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChecks\u003C/strong\u003E — design rules with KLayout and layout versus schematic with Netgen; every result is a row in Problems, with its cell and place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGate-level simulation\u003C/strong\u003E with the delays of an SDF file, and \u003Cstrong\u003Etest patterns\u003C/strong\u003E (STIL) for a tester.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-tools-run-as-separate-programs\"\u003EYour tools, run as separate programs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft ships none of these tools and no process design kit (PDK). You install them; CadCraft Studio runs them from the folder you set, each as a separate program that reads and writes files. That is how GPL tools are used too: they are never part of CadCraft and never linked into it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat for\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELicence (as its project publishes it)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStatus\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIcarus Verilog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esimulation, gate-level simulation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGPL-2.0-or-later\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erun by us on the sample design\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYosys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esynthesis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erun by us on the sample design\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVerilator\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efast simulation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELGPL-3.0 or Artistic-2.0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enot run by us yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpenROAD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eplace and route, timing, SDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBSD-3-Clause\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enot run by us yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKLayout\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edesign rule check\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGPL-2.0-or-later\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enot run by us yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENetgen\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elayout versus schematic\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGPL (see its own licence file)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enot run by us yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe browser edition reads and shows every file and result but cannot start programs: it saves a run package you run on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is-not\"\u003EWhat it is not\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor learning and open-silicon flows, not a signed-off tape-out system. We ship no PDK and make no foundry claims: open PDKs (for example SkyWater 130 or GF180) are your own download. The cell library in the sample design is made up for teaching.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Chip design is an add-on for CadCraft (Settings › Add-ons › Chip design; off until you switch it on). It keeps a chip design as a project: your Verilog or VHDL files, the stages of the flow, the tools and their versions, and for every run the SHA-256 of what went in and what came out, so you can see when a run would be the same and run it again. The flow Source — write and check Verilog or VHDL; errors at their line; the module Hierarchy. Simulation — your testbench with Icarus Verilog or Verilator; the waves in the Waves tab. Synthesis — Yosys maps the design onto a cell library (a Liberty file); cells, area and timing in Problems; the gate-level Netlist as a schematic. Place and route — OpenROAD with your LEF, Liberty and SDC; the Floorplan tab shows the DEF. Layout — GDSII and OASIS files, layers and cell hierarchy; Cell library and floorplan; Constraints and timing data (SDC, SPEF, SDF). Checks — design rules with KLayout and layout versus schematic with Netgen; every result is a row in Problems, with its cell and place. Gate-level simulation with the delays of an SDF file, and test patterns (STIL) for a tester. Your tools, run as separate programs CadCraft ships none of these tools and no process design kit (PDK). You install them; CadCraft Studio runs them from the folder you set, each as a separate program that reads and writes files. That is how GPL tools are used too: they are never part of CadCraft and never linked into it. Tool What for Licence (as its project publishes it) Status Icarus Verilog simulation, gate-level simulation GPL-2.0-or-later run by us on the sample design Yosys synthesis ISC run by us on the sample design Verilator fast simulation LGPL-3.0 or Artistic-2.0 not run by us yet OpenROAD place and route, timing, SDF BSD-3-Clause not run by us yet KLayout design rule check GPL-2.0-or-later not run by us yet Netgen layout versus schematic GPL (see its own licence file) not run by us yet The browser edition reads and shows every file and result but cannot start programs: it saves a run package you run on your computer. What it is not For learning and open-silicon flows, not a signed-off tape-out system. We ship no PDK and make no foundry claims: open PDKs (for example SkyWater 130 or GF180) are your own download. The cell library in the sample design is made up for teaching."},{"slug":"chip-sample","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: get started with the sample","summary":"Open Pebble, a small teaching processor, and walk through its source, netlist, layout, waves and problems.","section":"start","order":2,"keywords":["sample","tutorial","getting started","pebble","processor","cpu","risc","walkthrough","example design"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-sample","text":"Open the sample","level":2},{"id":"walk-through-it","text":"Walk through it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-sample\"\u003EOpen the sample\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESwitch on Settings › Add-ons › \u003Cstrong\u003EChip design\u003C/strong\u003E, open the \u003Cstrong\u003EChip design\u003C/strong\u003E section of the right panel (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHIP\u003C/code\u003E) and choose \u003Cstrong\u003ELarger window\u003C/strong\u003E for room. On the \u003Cstrong\u003EProject\u003C/strong\u003E tab choose \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen the sample design\u003C/strong\u003E. It opens \u003Cstrong\u003EPebble\u003C/strong\u003E: a small RISC-style processor written for CadCraft (16-bit instructions, four 8-bit registers, eight bytes of memory, a short program that adds 1 to 10, prints seven Fibonacci numbers and stops), its testbench, the made-up teaching cell library \u003Ccode\u003Ecc_cells\u003C/code\u003E (Liberty, LEF and simulation models) and the timing constraints \u003Ccode\u003Epebble.sdc\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sample also holds the results of real runs we made: Icarus Verilog simulated it, Yosys synthesized it onto \u003Ccode\u003Ecc_cells\u003C/code\u003E (801 cells), and a gate-level simulation ran with an SDF. Its placement (\u003Ccode\u003Eout/layout/pebble.def\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Epebble.gds\u003C/code\u003E) was made by our own sample script, not by a place-and-route tool, and has no routing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walk-through-it\"\u003EWalk through it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESource\u003C/strong\u003E — pick \u003Ccode\u003Epebble_core.v\u003C/code\u003E. The checker finds no errors. Change a line to see one appear at its place, then undo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHierarchy\u003C/strong\u003E — \u003Ccode\u003Epebble_soc\u003C/code\u003E holds the program memory and the core; the core holds the registers, the ALU and the data memory.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENetlist\u003C/strong\u003E — pick \u003Ccode\u003Epebble_soc\u003C/code\u003E from \u003Ccode\u003Eout/synth/netlist.v\u003C/code\u003E to see the synthesized gates as a schematic; click a wire to light its net.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayout\u003C/strong\u003E — open \u003Ccode\u003Eout/layout/pebble.gds\u003C/code\u003E: the cells in their rows; zoom with the wheel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECell library and floorplan\u003C/strong\u003E — \u003Ccode\u003Ecc_cells.lib\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Ecc_cells.lef\u003C/code\u003E side by side; open \u003Ccode\u003Eout/layout/pebble.def\u003C/code\u003E for the floorplan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConstraints and timing data\u003C/strong\u003E — \u003Ccode\u003Epebble.sdc\u003C/code\u003E in plain words; \u003Ccode\u003Eout/layout/pebble.sdf\u003C/code\u003E shows each cell&#039;s delay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWaves\u003C/strong\u003E — choose \u003Ccode\u003Eout/sim/wave.vcd\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003Eout/gls/gls.vcd\u003C/code\u003E, the gate-level run, where outputs change a little after each clock edge).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlow\u003C/strong\u003E — every stage with its command lines and the recorded runs. In Studio, with Icarus Verilog and Yosys installed, \u003Cstrong\u003ERun\u003C/strong\u003E does them again; in the browser, \u003Cstrong\u003ESave run package\u003C/strong\u003E and run it on your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProblems\u003C/strong\u003E — what the checks found shows in the Problems panel, one source per stage.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E","text":"Open the sample Switch on Settings › Add-ons › Chip design, open the Chip design section of the right panel (or type CHIP) and choose Larger window for room. On the Project tab choose Open the sample design. It opens Pebble: a small RISC-style processor written for CadCraft (16-bit instructions, four 8-bit registers, eight bytes of memory, a short program that adds 1 to 10, prints seven Fibonacci numbers and stops), its testbench, the made-up teaching cell library cc_cells (Liberty, LEF and simulation models) and the timing constraints pebble.sdc. The sample also holds the results of real runs we made: Icarus Verilog simulated it, Yosys synthesized it onto cc_cells (801 cells), and a gate-level simulation ran with an SDF. Its placement (out/layout/pebble.def, pebble.gds) was made by our own sample script, not by a place-and-route tool, and has no routing. Walk through it Source — pick pebble_core.v. The checker finds no errors. Change a line to see one appear at its place, then undo. Hierarchy — pebble_soc holds the program memory and the core; the core holds the registers, the ALU and the data memory. Netlist — pick pebble_soc from out/synth/netlist.v to see the synthesized gates as a schematic; click a wire to light its net. Layout — open out/layout/pebble.gds: the cells in their rows; zoom with the wheel. Cell library and floorplan — cc_cells.lib and cc_cells.lef side by side; open out/layout/pebble.def for the floorplan. Constraints and timing data — pebble.sdc in plain words; out/layout/pebble.sdf shows each cell's delay. Waves — choose out/sim/wave.vcd (or out/gls/gls.vcd, the gate-level run, where outputs change a little after each clock edge). Flow — every stage with its command lines and the recorded runs. In Studio, with Icarus Verilog and Yosys installed, Run does them again; in the browser, Save run package and run it on your computer. Problems — what the checks found shows in the Problems panel, one source per stage."},{"slug":"chip-source","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: Verilog and VHDL source","summary":"Write and check Verilog or VHDL, see errors at their line and column, and see which module uses which in the hierarchy.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["verilog","systemverilog","vhdl","hdl","rtl","syntax check","module","entity","hierarchy","top module","instance"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-source","text":"Open the source","level":2},{"id":"errors-and-warnings","text":"Errors and warnings","level":2},{"id":"the-hierarchy","text":"The hierarchy","level":2},{"id":"what-is-read","text":"What is read","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-supported","text":"What is not supported","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-source\"\u003EOpen the source\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESource\u003C/span\u003E. The list shows every \u003Ccode\u003E.v\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.sv\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.vh\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.vhd\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.vhdl\u003C/code\u003E file in the project. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew file\u003C/span\u003E starts an empty module (or entity, for a VHDL name). Type in the box; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E (or Ctrl+S) writes the file back to the project.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"errors-and-warnings\"\u003EErrors and warnings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe files are checked as you type. Each problem has a line and a column: a dot in the margin marks the line (point at it to read the message), and the list under the box takes you to the place when clicked. The same rows go to the Problems panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EErrors are things a tool would refuse: a missing \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Eend\u003C/code\u003E, a name used but never declared, a module or entity defined twice, an instance of something that is not in the project, a port that the module does not have. Warnings may be right on purpose: a module nothing uses, a port connected to the wrong number of bits. Notes say what was assumed, for example a name that becomes a one-bit wire because it was never declared.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-hierarchy\"\u003EThe hierarchy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHierarchy\u003C/span\u003E shows the top and everything under it, as instance name and module. The top is found by itself: the module that nothing else uses and that has the biggest tree under it (a test bench, a module with no ports, is shown on its own). Choose another in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E. Tags mark modules that are not in the project, modules nothing uses, and modules that end up using themselves. Click a row to go to its line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVHDL and Verilog can be mixed: a VHDL architecture can use a Verilog module and the other way round (names are matched without case for VHDL).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-read\"\u003EWhat is read\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVerilog: the synthesizable part of IEEE 1364-2005 (modules, ports in both styles, parameters, wires and regs, assign, always and initial blocks, if, case, for, functions and tasks, generate for and if, gate primitives, instances with named, positional and \u003Ccode\u003E.*\u003C/code\u003E connections), \u003Ccode\u003Edefine, \u003C/code\u003Eifdef, `include of a project file, and a few SystemVerilog words (logic, always_ff, always_comb, &#039;0 and &#039;1).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVHDL: library and use, entity with generics and ports, architecture with signals, constants, components and functions, processes, component and entity instances with generic map and port map, when/else and with/select assignments, for and if generate, blocks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-supported\"\u003EWhat is not supported\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo type checking (a VHDL signal of the wrong type is not found), no SystemVerilog classes, interfaces, packages, structs or enums (typedef lines are skipped), no generate case, no user-defined primitives (skipped with a note), no VHDL configurations (skipped with a note). This is a checker to catch mistakes early; your simulator and synthesis tool have the last word.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open the source Open Source. The list shows every .v, .sv, .vh, .vhd and .vhdl file in the project. New file starts an empty module (or entity, for a VHDL name). Type in the box; Save (or Ctrl+S) writes the file back to the project. Errors and warnings The files are checked as you type. Each problem has a line and a column: a dot in the margin marks the line (point at it to read the message), and the list under the box takes you to the place when clicked. The same rows go to the Problems panel. Errors are things a tool would refuse: a missing ; or end, a name used but never declared, a module or entity defined twice, an instance of something that is not in the project, a port that the module does not have. Warnings may be right on purpose: a module nothing uses, a port connected to the wrong number of bits. Notes say what was assumed, for example a name that becomes a one-bit wire because it was never declared. The hierarchy Hierarchy shows the top and everything under it, as instance name and module. The top is found by itself: the module that nothing else uses and that has the biggest tree under it (a test bench, a module with no ports, is shown on its own). Choose another in Top. Tags mark modules that are not in the project, modules nothing uses, and modules that end up using themselves. Click a row to go to its line. VHDL and Verilog can be mixed: a VHDL architecture can use a Verilog module and the other way round (names are matched without case for VHDL). What is read Verilog: the synthesizable part of IEEE 1364-2005 (modules, ports in both styles, parameters, wires and regs, assign, always and initial blocks, if, case, for, functions and tasks, generate for and if, gate primitives, instances with named, positional and .* connections), define, ifdef, `include of a project file, and a few SystemVerilog words (logic, always_ff, always_comb, '0 and '1). VHDL: library and use, entity with generics and ports, architecture with signals, constants, components and functions, processes, component and entity instances with generic map and port map, when/else and with/select assignments, for and if generate, blocks. What is not supported No type checking (a VHDL signal of the wrong type is not found), no SystemVerilog classes, interfaces, packages, structs or enums (typedef lines are skipped), no generate case, no user-defined primitives (skipped with a note), no VHDL configurations (skipped with a note). This is a checker to catch mistakes early; your simulator and synthesis tool have the last word."},{"slug":"chip-netlist","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: netlist and schematic","summary":"Turn a structural Verilog module into a netlist of cells, pins and nets, and look at it as a schematic.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["netlist","schematic","gate level","structural verilog","synthesis output","nets","cells","bus","driver"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-module","text":"Draw a module","level":2},{"id":"look-around","text":"Look around","level":2},{"id":"checks","text":"Checks","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-supported","text":"What is not supported","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-module\"\u003EDraw a module\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENetlist\u003C/span\u003E and pick a Verilog module in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModule\u003C/span\u003E (the top is picked first). The panel reads the module&#039;s instances, gates and \u003Ccode\u003Eassign\u003C/code\u003E lines and draws them: inputs on the left, outputs on the right, and each box in a column after the boxes that drive it. Wires leave each output and run to every input on the same net.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBuses are split into bits: \u003Ccode\u003Ea[3:0]\u003C/code\u003E becomes four nets \u003Ccode\u003Ea[3]\u003C/code\u003E … \u003Ccode\u003Ea[0]\u003C/code\u003E, so a box&#039;s pins are one bit each. Plain \u003Ccode\u003Eassign\u003C/code\u003E wiring (\u003Ccode\u003Eassign b = a;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eassign c = {a, 1&#039;b0};\u003C/code\u003E) joins nets instead of drawing a box; constants become tie boxes \u003Ccode\u003E1&#039;b0\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E1&#039;b1\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is meant for gate-level netlists, such as what a synthesis tool writes. A module with \u003Ccode\u003Ealways\u003C/code\u003E blocks or \u003Ccode\u003Eassign\u003C/code\u003E lines that compute something is still drawn, with one dashed box per block or line, so you can see what feeds what.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around\"\u003ELook around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScroll to zoom, drag to move, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to see it all. Click a wire to light its whole net and read its name; click a box to go to its line in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESource\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave picture (SVG)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave netlist (JSON)\u003C/span\u003E write files into the project.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"checks\"\u003EChecks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under the drawing (and the Problems panel) shows nets with more than one driver, inputs that nothing drives, inputs left open, and pins connected to the wrong number of bits. Where a cell is not a module of the project and no cell library is loaded, its pin directions are guessed from the pin names (Y, Q, Z and similar are outputs) and a note says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-supported\"\u003EWhat is not supported\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGenerate loops are not unrolled (instances inside them are left out, with a note), instance arrays are drawn as one box, and submodules are not flattened: pick a submodule in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModule\u003C/span\u003E to see inside it. Very large netlists (over 4,000 cells) are not drawn, but can still be saved as JSON.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Draw a module Open Netlist and pick a Verilog module in Module (the top is picked first). The panel reads the module's instances, gates and assign lines and draws them: inputs on the left, outputs on the right, and each box in a column after the boxes that drive it. Wires leave each output and run to every input on the same net. Buses are split into bits: a[3:0] becomes four nets a[3] … a[0], so a box's pins are one bit each. Plain assign wiring (assign b = a;, assign c = {a, 1'b0};) joins nets instead of drawing a box; constants become tie boxes 1'b0 and 1'b1. It is meant for gate-level netlists, such as what a synthesis tool writes. A module with always blocks or assign lines that compute something is still drawn, with one dashed box per block or line, so you can see what feeds what. Look around Scroll to zoom, drag to move, Fit to see it all. Click a wire to light its whole net and read its name; click a box to go to its line in Source. Save picture (SVG) and Save netlist (JSON) write files into the project. Checks The list under the drawing (and the Problems panel) shows nets with more than one driver, inputs that nothing drives, inputs left open, and pins connected to the wrong number of bits. Where a cell is not a module of the project and no cell library is loaded, its pin directions are guessed from the pin names (Y, Q, Z and similar are outputs) and a note says so. What is not supported Generate loops are not unrolled (instances inside them are left out, with a note), instance arrays are drawn as one box, and submodules are not flattened: pick a submodule in Module to see inside it. Very large netlists (over 4,000 cells) are not drawn, but can still be saved as JSON."},{"slug":"chip-run","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: run your own tools","summary":"Simulate, synthesize, place and route, check design rules and LVS with the tools you installed, and see the waves, the layout and the reports.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["chip","simulation","icarus","verilator","yosys","openroad","klayout","netgen","drc","lvs","waveform","vcd","def","sdf","stil","gate level"],"headings":[{"id":"where-it-is","text":"Where it is","level":2},{"id":"your-tools-nothing-bundled","text":"Your tools, nothing bundled","level":2},{"id":"what-comes-back","text":"What comes back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-is\"\u003EWhere it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESwitch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons (Chip design), then open the \u003Cstrong\u003EChip design\u003C/strong\u003E section of the right panel (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHIP\u003C/code\u003E). \u003Cstrong\u003ELarger window\u003C/strong\u003E opens the same panel in a big dialog. The \u003Cstrong\u003EFlow\u003C/strong\u003E tab lists the stages of your project: simulation, synthesis, place and route, design rule check, layout vs schematic, gate-level simulation and test patterns.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-tools-nothing-bundled\"\u003EYour tools, nothing bundled\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft ships no simulator, synthesis or layout tool and no process design kit (PDK). In \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E, a stage&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003ERun\u003C/strong\u003E starts the tools you installed yourself — Icarus Verilog, Verilator, Yosys, OpenROAD, KLayout, Netgen — as separate programs, from the \u003Cstrong\u003ETools folder\u003C/strong\u003E you set on the Project tab (empty = the search path). The first run asks you to allow Node to run the add-on&#039;s run script. \u003Cstrong\u003EFind my tools\u003C/strong\u003E records each tool&#039;s version in the project.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cstrong\u003Ebrowser edition\u003C/strong\u003E, nothing can start a program: \u003Cstrong\u003ESave run package\u003C/strong\u003E gives you a zip with the inputs, the steps and the run script. Unzip it and run \u003Ccode\u003Enode engine/cc-chip-run.mjs chip-job.json\u003C/code\u003E on your computer, then \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a result\u003C/strong\u003E with the \u003Ccode\u003Eout/result.zip\u003C/code\u003E it writes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-comes-back\"\u003EWhat comes back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMessages, errors, timing and rule violations go to \u003Cstrong\u003EProblems\u003C/strong\u003E, one source per stage. A design rule violation carries its cell and its place on the layout, in micrometres.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWaves\u003C/strong\u003E shows a VCD from the simulation (FST files are not read: ask for VCD).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFloorplan\u003C/strong\u003E shows the DEF from place and route: floorplan, placement and routing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery run keeps the SHA-256 of its inputs and outputs and the tool&#039;s version in the project, and the Flow tab says when the inputs changed since the last run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor learning and open-silicon flows, not a signed-off tape-out system.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Where it is Switch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons (Chip design), then open the Chip design section of the right panel (or type CHIP). Larger window opens the same panel in a big dialog. The Flow tab lists the stages of your project: simulation, synthesis, place and route, design rule check, layout vs schematic, gate-level simulation and test patterns. Your tools, nothing bundled CadCraft ships no simulator, synthesis or layout tool and no process design kit (PDK). In CadCraft Studio, a stage's Run starts the tools you installed yourself — Icarus Verilog, Verilator, Yosys, OpenROAD, KLayout, Netgen — as separate programs, from the Tools folder you set on the Project tab (empty = the search path). The first run asks you to allow Node to run the add-on's run script. Find my tools records each tool's version in the project. In the browser edition, nothing can start a program: Save run package gives you a zip with the inputs, the steps and the run script. Unzip it and run node engine/cc-chip-run.mjs chip-job.json on your computer, then Open a result with the out/result.zip it writes. What comes back Messages, errors, timing and rule violations go to Problems, one source per stage. A design rule violation carries its cell and its place on the layout, in micrometres. Waves shows a VCD from the simulation (FST files are not read: ask for VCD). Floorplan shows the DEF from place and route: floorplan, placement and routing. Every run keeps the SHA-256 of its inputs and outputs and the tool's version in the project, and the Flow tab says when the inputs changed since the last run. For learning and open-silicon flows, not a signed-off tape-out system."},{"slug":"chip-layout","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: view and convert a layout (GDSII, OASIS)","summary":"Open a GDSII or OASIS layout, show and hide layers, pick a cell from the hierarchy, move around a big layout quickly and save it in the other format.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["gds","gdsii","gds2","oasis","oas","layout","mask","layer map","layer","datatype","hierarchy","cell","top cell","array","aref","sref","convert","stream"],"headings":[{"id":"open-a-layout","text":"Open a layout","level":2},{"id":"layers","text":"Layers","level":2},{"id":"cells-and-the-top-cell","text":"Cells and the top cell","level":2},{"id":"move-around","text":"Move around","level":2},{"id":"save-in-the-other-format","text":"Save in the other format","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"open-a-layout\"\u003EOpen a layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E panel and choose a \u003Ccode\u003E.gds\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.oas\u003C/code\u003E file in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout file\u003C/span\u003E. The file is read on your computer; nothing is sent anywhere. The kind is found from the file&#039;s first bytes, so a file with an odd name still opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line under the view says how many cells were read and how long it took. Anything odd in the file (a cell placed but never defined, a record cut short, a damaged checksum in an OASIS file) is listed in Problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"layers\"\u003ELayers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E lists every layer and datatype the layout uses, with two counts: how many shapes are stored in the file, and how many there are once every placement is counted (&quot;placed&quot;). Untick a layer to hide it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayers have no names in a GDSII file, only numbers. To name and colour them, choose a layer map in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer map file\u003C/span\u003E. Two kinds are read:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea plain text file, one layer a line, in any of these forms: \u003Ccode\u003E1/0 : NWELL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMETAL1 5/0 #2e6fd1 drawing\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EVIA1 6 0\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003Emetal1 drawing 15 0\u003C/code\u003E (name, purpose, layer, datatype). Lines starting with \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E//\u003C/code\u003E are comments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea CadCraft layer map (\u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E, format \u003Ccode\u003Ecc-layer-map\u003C/code\u003E), the same kind the PCB tools use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn OASIS file can carry its own layer names; they are used until you choose a map. XML layer property files from other programs are not read: export a plain text map from them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cells-and-the-top-cell\"\u003ECells and the top cell\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECells\u003C/span\u003E shows the cell hierarchy from the top cell (the cell nothing else places). The number beside a cell is how many times its parent places it, arrays counted. Choose a cell&#039;s name to make it the top of the view. A cell that places itself through other cells is marked and the loop is cut.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-around\"\u003EMove around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag to move; scroll to zoom about the pointer; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E shows the whole cell. Big layouts stay quick:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eplacements outside the window are skipped, and of an array only the rows and columns in view are drawn;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea cell smaller than a few pixels is drawn as its outline; a row of tiny cells is one outline;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea big flat cell seen from far away is drawn as shaded squares, darker where it holds more shapes;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea cell that is wholly in view is drawn once and reused at each of its placements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line under the view says what was drawn. If a view holds more than it can draw it says so; zoom in to see the rest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn our test layout of 1,000,960 stored shapes (20.2 million once every placement is counted) the whole-layout view is planned in about 20 ms after reading; reading takes about 0.3 s for the 62 MB GDSII file and 0.7 s for the 9.5 MB OASIS file on our server.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-in-the-other-format\"\u003ESave in the other format\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as GDSII\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as OASIS\u003C/span\u003E write the open layout next to the original, with the same name. OASIS files are compressed and carry a CRC32 checksum. Converting keeps every shape, path, placement, array, text and property, with these differences:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGDSII has no repeated shapes: a repeated shape from an OASIS file is written out one copy at a time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOASIS texts have no turn, size or mirror: those are dropped. GDSII BOX elements become polygons. Paths with round ends become paths with square ends half a width long.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA GDSII polygon can have at most 8,191 corners; a bigger one is left out and named in Problems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:chip/chip-formats-limits\" data-topic=\"addon:chip/chip-formats-limits\"\u003EChip design: limits of the file formats\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open a layout Open the Layout panel and choose a .gds or .oas file in Layout file. The file is read on your computer; nothing is sent anywhere. The kind is found from the file's first bytes, so a file with an odd name still opens. The line under the view says how many cells were read and how long it took. Anything odd in the file (a cell placed but never defined, a record cut short, a damaged checksum in an OASIS file) is listed in Problems. Layers Layers lists every layer and datatype the layout uses, with two counts: how many shapes are stored in the file, and how many there are once every placement is counted (\"placed\"). Untick a layer to hide it. Layers have no names in a GDSII file, only numbers. To name and colour them, choose a layer map in Layer map file. Two kinds are read: a plain text file, one layer a line, in any of these forms: 1/0 : NWELL, METAL1 5/0 #2e6fd1 drawing, VIA1 6 0, or metal1 drawing 15 0 (name, purpose, layer, datatype). Lines starting with # or // are comments. a CadCraft layer map (.json, format cc-layer-map), the same kind the PCB tools use. An OASIS file can carry its own layer names; they are used until you choose a map. XML layer property files from other programs are not read: export a plain text map from them. Cells and the top cell Cells shows the cell hierarchy from the top cell (the cell nothing else places). The number beside a cell is how many times its parent places it, arrays counted. Choose a cell's name to make it the top of the view. A cell that places itself through other cells is marked and the loop is cut. Move around Drag to move; scroll to zoom about the pointer; Fit shows the whole cell. Big layouts stay quick: placements outside the window are skipped, and of an array only the rows and columns in view are drawn; a cell smaller than a few pixels is drawn as its outline; a row of tiny cells is one outline; a big flat cell seen from far away is drawn as shaded squares, darker where it holds more shapes; a cell that is wholly in view is drawn once and reused at each of its placements. The line under the view says what was drawn. If a view holds more than it can draw it says so; zoom in to see the rest. On our test layout of 1,000,960 stored shapes (20.2 million once every placement is counted) the whole-layout view is planned in about 20 ms after reading; reading takes about 0.3 s for the 62 MB GDSII file and 0.7 s for the 9.5 MB OASIS file on our server. Save in the other format Save as GDSII and Save as OASIS write the open layout next to the original, with the same name. OASIS files are compressed and carry a CRC32 checksum. Converting keeps every shape, path, placement, array, text and property, with these differences: GDSII has no repeated shapes: a repeated shape from an OASIS file is written out one copy at a time. OASIS texts have no turn, size or mirror: those are dropped. GDSII BOX elements become polygons. Paths with round ends become paths with square ends half a width long. A GDSII polygon can have at most 8,191 corners; a bigger one is left out and named in Problems. See Chip design: limits of the file formats."},{"slug":"chip-library","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: cell libraries and floorplans (Liberty, LEF, DEF)","summary":"Read a cell library's timing, power and area, its cell outlines and pins, and see a placed and routed design's floorplan.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["liberty","lib","lef","def","standard cell","cell library","timing","nldm","lookup table","power","leakage","area","macro","pin","site","row","floorplan","placement","routing","net","special net"],"headings":[{"id":"open-library-and-design-files","text":"Open library and design files","level":2},{"id":"see-the-floorplan","text":"See the floorplan","level":2},{"id":"what-is-checked","text":"What is checked","level":2},{"id":"writing","text":"Writing","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"open-library-and-design-files\"\u003EOpen library and design files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECell library and floorplan\u003C/span\u003E panel and choose a file in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELiberty, LEF or DEF file\u003C/span\u003E. You can open one of each; they are joined by cell name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELiberty (\u003Ccode\u003E.lib\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/strong\u003E: every cell with its area, leakage power, pins (direction, capacitance, function), timing arcs (related pin, sense, type) and their lookup tables (delay and transition by input slew and output load), internal power and flip-flop or latch behaviour.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELEF (\u003Ccode\u003E.lef\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/strong\u003E: units, routing and cut layers (direction, pitch, width, spacing), vias, sites, and each cell&#039;s size, pins with their shapes and obstructions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDEF (\u003Ccode\u003E.def\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/strong\u003E: die area, rows, tracks, placed components, pins, nets and power nets with their routed wires.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table lists each cell with its area, size (from the LEF), leakage, pins and how many timing arcs it has.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-the-floorplan\"\u003ESee the floorplan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a LEF and then a DEF. The view shows the die outline, rows, every placed cell at its real size (fixed cells in red), pins, signal wires (green) and power wires (yellow) at their widths. Cells the LEF does not describe are drawn as 1 µm squares and counted under the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-checked\"\u003EWhat is checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EProblems lists: tables whose size does not match their indexes, indexes that do not increase, cells with no area, outputs with no timing arc (Liberty); cells with no size and pins with no shape (LEF); nets that name a component or pin the design does not have, and sections whose count is wrong (DEF).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"writing\"\u003EWriting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe readers keep everything they read, including statements they do not model, so a file read and written again by the add-on reads back the same. The add-on uses this to hand files to the tools you run yourself.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open library and design files Open the Cell library and floorplan panel and choose a file in Liberty, LEF or DEF file. You can open one of each; they are joined by cell name. Liberty (.lib): every cell with its area, leakage power, pins (direction, capacitance, function), timing arcs (related pin, sense, type) and their lookup tables (delay and transition by input slew and output load), internal power and flip-flop or latch behaviour. LEF (.lef): units, routing and cut layers (direction, pitch, width, spacing), vias, sites, and each cell's size, pins with their shapes and obstructions. DEF (.def): die area, rows, tracks, placed components, pins, nets and power nets with their routed wires. The table lists each cell with its area, size (from the LEF), leakage, pins and how many timing arcs it has. See the floorplan Open a LEF and then a DEF. The view shows the die outline, rows, every placed cell at its real size (fixed cells in red), pins, signal wires (green) and power wires (yellow) at their widths. Cells the LEF does not describe are drawn as 1 µm squares and counted under the view. What is checked Problems lists: tables whose size does not match their indexes, indexes that do not increase, cells with no area, outputs with no timing arc (Liberty); cells with no size and pins with no shape (LEF); nets that name a component or pin the design does not have, and sections whose count is wrong (DEF). Writing The readers keep everything they read, including statements they do not model, so a file read and written again by the add-on reads back the same. The add-on uses this to hand files to the tools you run yourself."},{"slug":"chip-constraints","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: constraints, parasitics, delays and test patterns (SDC, SPEF, SDF, STIL)","summary":"List a design's timing constraints in plain words, check them against its pins, read parasitics and delays, and write test patterns for a tester.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["sdc","constraints","create_clock","clock","input delay","output delay","false path","multicycle","max delay","spef","parasitics","rc","capacitance","resistance","sdf","delay","back-annotation","stil","ieee 1450","test patterns","vectors","ate","tester"],"headings":[{"id":"constraints-sdc","text":"Constraints (SDC)","level":2},{"id":"parasitics-spef","text":"Parasitics (SPEF)","level":2},{"id":"delays-sdf","text":"Delays (SDF)","level":2},{"id":"test-patterns-stil","text":"Test patterns (STIL)","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"constraints-sdc\"\u003EConstraints (SDC)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstraints and timing data\u003C/span\u003E panel and choose an \u003Ccode\u003E.sdc\u003C/code\u003E file. Each constraint is listed in plain words with its line: clocks with period and edges, generated clocks, input and output delays, paths that are not timed or may take more cycles, delay limits, loads, driving cells and clock groups. Commands that are rarer are counted by name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file is read, never run. \u003Ccode\u003Eset name value\u003C/code\u003E is remembered so \u003Ccode\u003E$name\u003C/code\u003E reads as the value; everything else that is Tcl (\u003Ccode\u003Eif\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eforeach\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eexpr\u003C/code\u003E, procedures) is kept as written and listed in Problems as &quot;not run&quot;, so you know which lines the list does not reflect.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the design&#039;s DEF as well and every \u003Ccode\u003Eget_ports\u003C/code\u003E pattern is checked against its pins; a pattern that matches no pin is listed in Problems. Clock periods that are not above zero and delays that name a clock nobody made are listed too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"parasitics-spef\"\u003EParasitics (SPEF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose a \u003Ccode\u003E.spef\u003C/code\u003E file to see each net&#039;s total capacitance, how much goes to ground and how much couples to other nets, its resistance and how many nodes it has. Names written as numbers in the file are shown by their real names. A net whose capacitors do not add up to its stated total is listed in Problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delays-sdf\"\u003EDelays (SDF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose an \u003Ccode\u003E.sdf\u003C/code\u003E file to list its cell delays (from input to output pin, with edges and conditions), wire delays and added delays, with rise and fall at the typical corner. Timing checks (setup, hold, width) are read too. The simulation that uses these delays is run from the add-on&#039;s simulation panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"test-patterns-stil\"\u003ETest patterns (STIL)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETest patterns (STIL)\u003C/span\u003E, open the DEF first: its pins become the signals, in the order shown. Clocks from the SDC pulse once in each cycle. Type one vector a line, one character a signal: inputs \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E, clocks \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E (no pulse) or \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (pulse), outputs \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E (do not care) or \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E (expect off). Set the period and when outputs are compared, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave STIL file\u003C/span\u003E. The file has the signals, signal groups, one waveform table, a pattern burst and the pattern. Wrong characters and vectors of the wrong length are listed in Problems and nothing is saved.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Constraints (SDC) Open the Constraints and timing data panel and choose an .sdc file. Each constraint is listed in plain words with its line: clocks with period and edges, generated clocks, input and output delays, paths that are not timed or may take more cycles, delay limits, loads, driving cells and clock groups. Commands that are rarer are counted by name. The file is read, never run. set name value is remembered so $name reads as the value; everything else that is Tcl (if, foreach, expr, procedures) is kept as written and listed in Problems as \"not run\", so you know which lines the list does not reflect. Open the design's DEF as well and every get_ports pattern is checked against its pins; a pattern that matches no pin is listed in Problems. Clock periods that are not above zero and delays that name a clock nobody made are listed too. Parasitics (SPEF) Choose a .spef file to see each net's total capacitance, how much goes to ground and how much couples to other nets, its resistance and how many nodes it has. Names written as numbers in the file are shown by their real names. A net whose capacitors do not add up to its stated total is listed in Problems. Delays (SDF) Choose an .sdf file to list its cell delays (from input to output pin, with edges and conditions), wire delays and added delays, with rise and fall at the typical corner. Timing checks (setup, hold, width) are read too. The simulation that uses these delays is run from the add-on's simulation panel. Test patterns (STIL) Under Test patterns (STIL), open the DEF first: its pins become the signals, in the order shown. Clocks from the SDC pulse once in each cycle. Type one vector a line, one character a signal: inputs 0 or 1, clocks 0 (no pulse) or P (pulse), outputs L, H, X (do not care) or Z (expect off). Set the period and when outputs are compared, then Save STIL file. The file has the signals, signal groups, one waveform table, a pattern burst and the pattern. Wrong characters and vectors of the wrong length are listed in Problems and nothing is saved."},{"slug":"chip-formats-limits","scope":"addon:chip","title":"Chip design: limits of the file formats","summary":"What the chip file readers and writers do not do yet, and how far they have been tested.","section":"trouble","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not supported","oasis","gdsii","liberty","lef","def","sdc","spef","sdf","stil","wgl","ccs","ecsm"],"headings":[{"id":"how-these-were-made-and-tested","text":"How these were made and tested","level":2},{"id":"not-read-or-not-modelled","text":"Not read or not modelled","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-these-were-made-and-tested\"\u003EHow these were made and tested\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery reader and writer is our own, written from the published descriptions of the formats. They are tested with small sample files we wrote by hand, with round trips (read, write, read again, compare), against a second reader in CadCraft for GDSII, and against a standard compression library for OASIS. \u003Cstrong\u003EThey have not yet been tried on files from other programs or from a foundry&#039;s design kit.\u003C/strong\u003E If a file of yours does not read, the Problems panel says where reading stopped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-read-or-not-modelled\"\u003ENot read or not modelled\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELiberty\u003C/strong\u003E: composite current source and other current-based models are kept but not shown; only table lookups (delay, transition, constraint, power) are modelled. Bus pins are read; bundles are read as buses.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELEF / DEF\u003C/strong\u003E: properties, non-default rules, blockages, regions, groups, fills and scan chains are kept and written back but not drawn. Wires drawn with \u003Ccode\u003ERECT\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EVIRTUAL\u003C/code\u003E in DEF are kept, not drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESDC\u003C/strong\u003E: no Tcl is run (see the constraints topic). Only \u003Ccode\u003Eset\u003C/code\u003E with a plain value is used.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESPEF\u003C/strong\u003E: inductances are read but not used; pin loads are not added to the delay estimate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESDF\u003C/strong\u003E: constructs other than cell, wire, port and device delays and the usual timing checks are kept as written.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTIL\u003C/strong\u003E: only the writer, for one waveform table and one pattern. No other tester formats are written.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOASIS\u003C/strong\u003E: trapezoids are turned into polygons by our reading of the specification and circles into 64-sided polygons; extension records are skipped. The writer writes rectangles, polygons, paths, placements and texts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGDSII\u003C/strong\u003E: electrical nodes, fonts and reference libraries are skipped and counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayer maps\u003C/strong\u003E: XML layer property files are not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"How these were made and tested Every reader and writer is our own, written from the published descriptions of the formats. They are tested with small sample files we wrote by hand, with round trips (read, write, read again, compare), against a second reader in CadCraft for GDSII, and against a standard compression library for OASIS. They have not yet been tried on files from other programs or from a foundry's design kit. If a file of yours does not read, the Problems panel says where reading stopped. Not read or not modelled Liberty: composite current source and other current-based models are kept but not shown; only table lookups (delay, transition, constraint, power) are modelled. Bus pins are read; bundles are read as buses. LEF / DEF: properties, non-default rules, blockages, regions, groups, fills and scan chains are kept and written back but not drawn. Wires drawn with RECT or VIRTUAL in DEF are kept, not drawn. SDC: no Tcl is run (see the constraints topic). Only set with a plain value is used. SPEF: inductances are read but not used; pin loads are not added to the delay estimate. SDF: constructs other than cell, wire, port and device delays and the usual timing checks are kept as written. STIL: only the writer, for one waveform table and one pattern. No other tester formats are written. OASIS: trapezoids are turned into polygons by our reading of the specification and circles into 64-sided polygons; extension records are skipped. The writer writes rectangles, polygons, paths, placements and texts. GDSII: electrical nodes, fonts and reference libraries are skipped and counted. Layer maps: XML layer property files are not read."}]};
